[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Typing Props with TypeScript in React"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript brings static typing to JavaScript, enhancing code maintainability and reducing runtime errors. In React, TypeScript is especially useful for defining the shape of your component's props, ensuring that components receive the data they expect."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Prop Typing"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The simplest way to type props is by defining an interface or a type alias that describes the structure of the props object. Let's start with a basic example:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"interface GreetingProps {
  name: string;
}

const Greeting: React.FC<GreetingProps> = ({ name }) => {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
};

export default Greeting;"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `GreetingProps` is an interface that specifies that the `Greeting` component expects a prop named `name` of type `string`. `React.FC<GreetingProps>` is a generic type that tells TypeScript that this is a functional component that accepts `GreetingProps`."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Optional Props"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes, you might want to make a prop optional. You can do this by adding a question mark `?` to the property name in the interface."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"interface ProfileProps {
  name: string;
  age?: number;
}

const Profile: React.FC<ProfileProps> = ({ name, age }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Name: {name}</p>
      {age && <p>Age: {age}</p>}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Profile;"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `age` is an optional prop. If it's not provided, it will be `undefined`.  The `age && <p>Age: {age}</p>` ensures that the age is only rendered if it's provided."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Default Props"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can provide default values for props using `defaultProps`. Note that you still need to define the prop as optional in the interface."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"interface ButtonProps {
  label: string;
  onClick: () => void;
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
}

const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ label, onClick, variant }) => {
  return <button onClick={onClick} className={`button ${variant}`}>{label}</button>;
};

Button.defaultProps = {
  variant: 'primary',
};

export default Button;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this case, if the `variant` prop is not provided, it defaults to `'primary'`. TypeScript knows that `variant` can be either `'primary'`, `'secondary'`, or `undefined` due to the optional type definition.  Using `defaultProps` is a legacy pattern and is generally discouraged.  Consider using default parameters in the function signature instead."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"interface ButtonProps {
  label: string;
  onClick: () => void;
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
}

const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ label, onClick, variant = 'primary' }) => {
  return <button onClick={onClick} className={`button ${variant}`}>{label}</button>;
};

export default Button;"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example shows the preferred way to set default props using default parameters in the function signature. If `variant` is not provided, it will default to 'primary'."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Prop Types: Union Types and Literal Types"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use union types to specify that a prop can be one of several types. Literal types allow you to restrict a prop to a specific set of values."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"interface StatusProps {
  status: 'success' | 'error' | 'pending';
  message: string | number;
}

const Status: React.FC<StatusProps> = ({ status, message }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Status: {status}</p>
      <p>Message: {message}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Status;"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `status` can only be one of `'success'`, `'error'`, or `'pending'`, and `message` can be either a `string` or a `number`."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Typing Event Handlers"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a prop is an event handler, you should type it accordingly using TypeScript's event types."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"interface InputProps {
  onChange: (event: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => void;
}

const Input: React.FC<InputProps> = ({ onChange }) => {
  return <input type="text" onChange={onChange} />;
};

export default Input;"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"`React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>` specifies the type of the event object for an input element's `onChange` event.  The `onChange` prop is a function that takes this event object as an argument and returns `void`."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Children Props"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"If your component renders its children, you can type the `children` prop using `React.ReactNode`."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"interface ContainerProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

const Container: React.FC<ContainerProps> = ({ children }) => {
  return <div className="container">{children}</div>;
};

export default Container;"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"`React.ReactNode` represents any value that can be rendered by React, including elements, strings, numbers, and fragments."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Type Aliases"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of interfaces, you can use type aliases to define prop types. The choice between interfaces and type aliases is largely a matter of preference, but interfaces are generally preferred for public APIs because they support declaration merging."},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"type CardProps = {
  title: string;
  description: string;
};

const Card: React.FC<CardProps> = ({ title, description }) => {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <p>{description}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Card;"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example uses a type alias `CardProps` to define the props for the `Card` component."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Components"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can create generic components that accept props with generic types. This is useful when you want to create reusable components that can work with different types of data."},{"order":35,"type":"code","text":"interface ListProps<T> {
  items: T[];
  renderItem: (item: T) => React.ReactNode;
}

const List = <T extends unknown>({ items, renderItem }: ListProps<T>) => {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item, index) => (
        <li key={index}>{renderItem(item)}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
};

export default List;"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `List` is a generic component that accepts an array of items of type `T` and a function `renderItem` that renders each item. The `T extends unknown` ensures that `T` is a valid type. This allows the `List` component to be used with arrays of strings, numbers, objects, or any other type."},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Typing props with TypeScript in React enhances code quality and helps prevent errors. By defining the structure of your component's props, you can ensure that your components receive the data they expect, leading to more robust and maintainable applications. This tutorial covered basic prop typing, optional props, default props, union types, literal types, event handlers, children props, type aliases, and generic components. Use these techniques to improve your React components with TypeScript."}]
